      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML Certificate of Conformity document.\n\n**Certificate level:**\n- number: R76/2006-GB1-12.05\n- Revision 2\n- scheme: this is a \"standard\" OIML CS certificate, not MAA... wait, it says \"OIML R76 MAA Rev. 2\" on page footer. Actually the footer says \"OIML R76 MAA Rev. 2 22 August 2014\". Hmm, but the certificate says \"OIML Certificate of Conformity\". The scheme field is (A|B). MAA = Mutual Acceptance Arrangement. The schema says scheme (A|B). For MAA certificates... Actually OIML CS has scheme A (basic) and B (MAA). Since this has MAA logo and \"MUTUAL ACCEPTANCE ARRANGEMENT\" emblem, scheme would be \"B\". Let me check: The MAA logo is present (\"OIML globe emblem with the text 'MUTUAL ACCEPTANCE ARRANGEMENT'\"). The footer says \"OIML R76 MAA Rev. 2\". So scheme: \"B\".\n\n- project_number: not mentioned explicitly. Reference №: TS1201/0041 — that's a reference number, could be project_number? The schema says project_number. Hmm. NMO certs often have a \"Reference №\". I'll not include it as project_number... Actually maybe include reference TS1201/0041 somewhere. The schema doesn't have a field for reference number at certificate level. I could omit or put in project_number. I think TS1201/0041 is an internal reference; safer to omit project_number since not clearly labeled. Actually, \"Reference №: TS1201/0041\" - this could be the project number for the evaluation. Hmm. The test report is P01332. The Reference № TS1201/0041 might be the testing reference. I'll include project_number: \"TS1201/0041\"? That's a stretch. Let me keep it safe and include it as project_number since it's the only reference number candidate. Actually rule 8: don't fabricate. project_number is not present. I'll omit it. Hmm, but then TS1201/0041 gets lost. There's no field for it. Maybe include in test_reports? The evaluation report is P01332 with 13 pages, role: type_evaluation_report. TS1201/0041 is the certificate's internal reference. I'll skip it or... I could put it in project_number. Many extractors would. I'll include it as project_number since it's labeled \"Reference №\" and it's the closest field. Hmm, actually I'd rather not fabricate semantics. Let me think again... The schema: certificate: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. There's no reference_number field. TS1201/0041 is a UKAS/NMO test reference. I think putting it in project_number is reasonable-ish but could be wrong. I'll omit to be safe? Actually the instruction \"Don't fabricate\" means don't make things up, but mapping \"Reference №\" to project_number is an interpretation. I'll include it — it preserves information. Hmm, risk either way. I'll include as project_number.\n\nWait — page_total: the certificate says \"This certificate includes 5 pages\" but the markdown shows pages 1-7 (page numbering weird; last page says Page 7 with \"includes 5 pages\" — OCR artifact). The stated total is 5 pages. But there are 7 pages in the markdown. The document says \"This certificate includes 5 pages\". Hmm, contradiction due to OCR. I'll go with page_total: 5 as stated? Or 7 as observed? The stated value is 5. Actually the certificate text repeatedly says \"This certificate includes 5 pages\" — that's what the document claims. I'll use 5.\n\n- member_state: United Kingdom (of Great Britain and Northern Ireland)\n- date_issued: 22 August 2014 (Revision 2 issue date)\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present. Omit.\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- name: National Measurement Office\n- address_lines: Stanton Avenue, Teddington, TW11 0JZ, United Kingdom\n- person_responsible: Paul Dixon\n- person_title: Director, Certification Services\n- phone: +44 (0)20 8943 7272\n- fax: not in schema (skip)\n- website: www.bis.gov.uk/nmo\n- email: not present\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present\n\n**Applicants:**\n- Avery Weigh-Tronix, Foundry Lane, Smethwick, West Midlands B66 2LP, United Kingdom\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- \"The applicant\" → same as applicant: Avery Weigh-Tronix with same address.\n\n**Certified type:**\n- category: \"Non-Automatic Weighing Instrument\" — the schema example list includes \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", ... For checkweigher it's an NAWI. Category could be \"Checkweigher\" or \"Non-Automatic Weighing Instrument\". I'll use \"Non-Automatic Weighing Instrument (Checkweigher)\"? Better: category \"Checkweigher\"? The instrument is designated ZQ375 Checkweigher, which is a NAWI. I'll put category: \"Non-Automatic Weighing Instrument (Checkweigher)\". Hmm, keep simple: \"Non-Automatic Weighing Instrument\". Actually the schema says category # \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\", ... — free to choose. I'll use \"Non-Automatic Weighing Instrument (Checkweigher)\".\n\n- type_designations: [\"ZQ375\"]. Also models: ZQ375 with Torsion base and Diamond base. Base models: BSF-99, BSF-1214, BSG-99, BSG-1214, BS-1824, BS-2020, BS-2424.\n\n- module_designation: The certificate mentions indicator ZQ375 (described in R76/2006-GB1-12.04) and load cells. For a complete instrument, module_designation... \"Not applicable\"? The instrument comprises indicating device and bases. I could leave null. Hmm. The instrument is a complete NAWI, not a module. I'd set \"Not applicable\"? Or null. I'll omit / null. Actually schema: module_designation # \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. For complete instruments, \"Not applicable\" fits. I'll use \"Not applicable\".\n\n- description: The family of instruments designated ZQ375 Checkweigher, comprises Torsion base and Diamond base models; Class III, mains or battery-powered, self-indicating NAWIs, not designed for direct sales to the public. Plus main features.\n\n**model_family:**\n- family_name: \"ZQ375 Checkweigher\" (explicitly \"This family of instruments is designated the ZQ375 Checkweigher\")\n- models: this is tricky. Variants: base models × capacities. There are many configurations. Options: one model per base configuration: BSF-99-3, BSF-99-6 (10kg cell), BSF-99-6 (20kg cell), BSF-1214-15 (6kg), BSF-1214-15 (15kg), BSF-1214-30, BSF-1214-45, BSF-1214-60, BSG-99-6(3kg), BSG-99-6(6kg), BSG-1214-15(6), BSG-1214-15(15), BSG-1214-30, BS-1824-45(30), BS-1824-45(45), BS-2020-50(30), BS-2020-45, BS-2020-50(45), BS-2020-50(50), BS-2020-100(60), BS-2020-100(90), BS-2020-100(100), BS-2424-90, BS-2424-150, BS-2424-200(FLS R60), BS-2424-200(FLS 1000lb), BS-2424-250.\n\nThat's a lot. The matrix tables handle the details. For model_family.models, I could list base models as variants: model_id like \"BSF-99-3\", etc. Let me enumerate carefully from tables:\n\nBSF-99: columns: BSF-99-3, BSF-99-6, BSF-99-6 (three configs)\n- config1: LC PW15AHC3MR/10kg, Max 3kg, Min 20g, e=1g, T≤3kg, Emax 10kg\n- config2: LC PW15AHC3MR/10kg, Max 6kg, Min 40g, e=2g, T≤6kg, Emax 10kg\n- config3: LC PW15AHC3MR/20kg, Max 6kg, Min 40g, e=2g, T≤6kg, Emax 20kg\n\nBSF-1214: BSF-1214-15, BSF-1214-15, BSF-1214-30, BSF-1214-45, BSF-1214-60\n- config1: PW15AHC3MR/20kg, Max 6, Min 40g, e 2g, T 6, Emax 20\n- config2: PW15AHC3MR/20kg, Max 15, Min 100g, e 5g, T 15, Emax 20\n- config3: PW15AHC3MR/50kg, Max 30, Min 200g, e 10g, T 30, Emax 50\n- config4: PW15AHC3MR/100kg, Max 45, Min 400g, e 20g, T 45, Emax 100\n- config5: PW15AHC3MR/100kg, Max 60, Min 400g, e 20g, T 60, Emax 100\n\nBSG-99: BSG-99-6, BSG-99-6\n- config1: Vishay 1130-10kg, Max 3, Min 20g, e 1g, T 3, Emax 10\n- config2: 1130-10kg, Max 6, Min 40g, e 2g, T 6, Emax 10\n\nBSG-1214: BSG-1214-15, BSG-1214-15, BSG-1214-30\n- config1: 1130-20kg, Max 6, Min 40g, e 2g, T 6, Emax 20\n- config2: 1130-20kg, Max 15, Min 100g, e 5g, T 15, Emax 20\n- config3: 1130-50kg, Max 30, Min 200g, e 10g, T 30, Emax 50\n\nBS-1824 (1824 Diamond base): BS-1824-45, BS-1824-45\n- config1: FLS R60 59.5kg, Max 30, Min 200g, e 10g, T 30, Emax 59.5\n- config2: FLS R60 59.5kg, Max 45, Min 200g, e 10g, T 45, Emax 59.5\n\nBS-2020 (2020 Diamond base): 7 columns: BS-2020-50, BS-2020-45, BS-2020-50 (colspan 2), BS-2020-100 (colspan 3)\nWait: columns listed: BS-2020-50 | BS-2020-45 | BS-2020-50 (colspan=2) | BS-2020-100 (colspan=3). That's 1+1+2+3 = 7 columns matching 7 load cell entries and 7 Max values.\n\nLoad cell models: FLS R60 59.5kg | FLS 125 lb | FLS R60 59.5kg | FLS R60 59.5kg | FLS R60 119kg | FLS R60 119kg | FLS R60 119kg\nMax: 30 | 45 | 45 | 50 | 60 | 90 | 100\nMin: 200 all\ne: 10 all\nT: 30,45,45,50,60,90,100\nEmax: 59.5 | 59.7 | 59.5 | 59.5 | 119 | 119 | 119\n\nBS-2424 (2424 Diamond base): BS-2424-90, BS-2424-150, BS-2424-200, BS-2424-200, BS-2424-250\nLoad cells: FLS 250 lb | FLS R60 299kg | FLS R60 299kg | FLS 1 000 lb | FLS R60 299kg\nMax: 90,150,200,200,250\nMin: 400,1000,1000,1000,1000\ne: 20,50,50,50,50\nT: same as Max\nEmax: 113,299,299,440,299\n\nSo I need to decide: model_level entries per base configuration, with matrix_tables preserving the tables.\n\nApproach: \n- model_family.models: list each configuration with model_id combining base model and Max, e.g. \"BSF-99-3/3kg\"? Hmm. Or simply base model with index. Given duplicates (BSF-99-6 appears twice with different configs), I need distinguishing IDs. I could use model_id like \"BSF-99-3 (Max 3 kg)\", \"BSF-99-6 (Max 6 kg, Emax 10 kg)\", \"BSF-99-6 (Max 6 kg, Emax 20 kg)\". Alternatively keep models as base models only: BSF-99, BSF-1214, BSG-99, BSG-1214, BS-1824, BS-2020, BS-2424 — but then capacities vary within, which would make model_level per base model. That's cleaner! Since each base model has multiple capacity variants, treat base model as the model and capacities as config? Hmm.\n\nActually the natural grouping: the family is ZQ375; models are the base configurations. The varying attributes are Max, Min, e, T, load cell. I'll do model_level with axis = base configuration.\n\nOption A: model_id per base model (7 models), and each model has multiple capacity variants → config_level with axis \"capacity variant\".\n\nOption B: model_id per configuration (27 configs) — heavy but complete.\n\nThe schema's model_level: attribute × model matrix with values [{model, value}]. With 27 configurations, each attribute gets 27 values. That's verbose but fine and unambiguous. But model_ids need uniqueness.\n\nLet me construct config IDs: For duplicates, append Max: \n- BSF-99: \"BSF-99-3 (Max 3 kg)\", \"BSF-99-6 (Max 6 kg, LC 10 kg)\", \"BSF-99-6 (Max 6 kg, LC 20 kg)\". Hmm clunky.\n\nAlternative: use base model + Max as model_id: \"BSF-99-3 / 3 kg\", duplicates: \"BSF-99-6 / 6 kg (PW15AHC3MR/10 kg)\" vs \"BSF-99-6 / 6 kg (PW15AHC3MR/20 kg)\". The Emax differs (10 vs 20), which reflects the load cell.\n\nLet me simplify: model_id = base model with capacity suffix where needed:\n1. \"BSF-99-3 (3 kg)\" — LC PW15AHC3MR/10 kg\n2. \"BSF-99-6 (6 kg, Emax 10 kg)\"\n3. \"BSF-99-6 (6 kg, Emax 20 kg)\"\n\nHmm, that embeds attributes in the ID. Common practice in these extractions: model_id like \"BSF-99-3\". For the duplicate BSF-99-6, maybe the second is actually a different Max? No — both Max 6. Actually looking at BSF-99 table: columns BSF-99-3, BSF-99-6, BSF-99-6 with Max 3, 6, 6 and load cells 10kg, 10kg, 20kg. Possibly OCR duplication of the middle column; but we must preserve as-is. \n\nI'll go with including the load cell in the model_id for disambiguation: e.g. \"BSF-99-6 (PW15AHC3MR/10 kg)\".\n\nFull model list (27):\nTorsion BSF-99 (HBM PW15AHC3MR):\n1. BSF-99-3 | LC PW15AHC3MR/10 kg | Max 3 kg | Min 20 g | e 1 g | T 3 kg | Emax 10 kg\n2. BSF-99-6 | LC PW15AHC3MR/10 kg | Max 6 kg | Min 40 g | e 2 g | T 6 kg | Emax 10 kg\n3. BSF-99-6 | LC PW15AHC3MR/20 kg | Max 6 kg | Min 40 g | e 2 g | T 6 kg | Emax 20 kg\n\nBSF-1214:\n4. BSF-1214-15 | LC /20 kg | Max 6 | Min 40 g | e 2 | T 6 | Emax 20\n5. BSF-1214-15 | LC /20 kg | Max 15 | Min 100 g | e 5 | T 15 | Emax 20\n6. BSF-1214-30 | LC /50 kg | Max 30 | Min 200 g | e 10 | T 30 | Emax 50\n7. BSF-1214-45 | LC /100 kg | Max 45 | Min 400 g | e 20 | T 45 | Emax 100\n8. BSF-1214-60 | LC /100 kg | Max 60 | Min 400 g | e 20 | T 60 | Emax 100\n\nBSG-99 (Vishay 1130):\n9. BSG-99-6 | 1130-10 kg | Max 3 | Min 20 g | e 1 | T 3 | Emax 10\n10. BSG-99-6 | 1130-10 kg | Max 6 | Min 40 g | e 2 | T 6 | Emax 10\n\nBSG-1214:\n11. BSG-1214-15 | 1130-20 kg | Max 6 | Min 40 | e 2 | T 6 | Emax 20\n12. BSG-1214-15 | 1130-20 kg | Max 15 | Min 100 | e 5 | T 15 | Emax 20\n13. BSG-1214-30 | 1130-50 kg | Max 30 | Min 200 | e 10 | T 30 | Emax 50\n\nBS-1824:\n14. BS-1824-45 | FLS R60 59.5 kg | Max 30 | Min 200 | e 10 | T 30 | Emax 59.5\n15. BS-1824-45 | FLS R60 59.5 kg | Max 45 | Min 200 | e 10 | T 45 | Emax 59.5\n\nBS-2020:\n16. BS-2020-50 | FLS R60 59.5 kg | Max 30 | Min 200 | e 10 | T 30 | Emax 59.5\n17. BS-2020-45 | FLS 125 lb | Max 45 | Min 200 | e 10 | T 45 | Emax 59.7\n18. BS-2020-50 | FLS R60 59.5 kg | Max 45 | Min 200 | e 10 | T 45 | Emax 59.5\n19. BS-2020-50 | FLS R60 59.5 kg | Max 50 | Min 200 | e 10 | T 50 | Emax 59.5\n20. BS-2020-100 | FLS R60 119 kg | Max 60 | Min 400 | e 20 | T 60 | Emax 119\n21. BS-2020-100 | FLS R60 119 kg | Max 90 | Min 400 | e 20 | T 90 | Emax 119\n22. BS-2020-100 | FLS R60 119 kg | Max 100 | Min 400 | e 20 | T 100 | Emax 119\n\nBS-2424:\n23. BS-2424-90 | FLS 250 lb | Max 90 | Min 400 | e 20 | T 90 | Emax 113\n24. BS-2424-150 | FLS R60 299 kg | Max 150 | Min 1000 | e 50 | T 150 | Emax 299\n25. BS-2424-200 | FLS R60 299 kg | Max 200 | Min 1000 | e 50 | T 200 | Emax 299\n26. BS-2424-200 | FLS 1000 lb | Max 200 | Min 1000 | e 50 | T 200 | Emax 440\n27. BS-2424-250 | FLS R60 299 kg | Max 250 | Min 1000 | e 50 | T 250 | Emax 299\n\nThat's 27 configurations. Building model_level matrices with 27 values per attribute × 5 attributes = 135 entries. That's heavy but machine-friendly. Alternatively, present matrix_tables for the 7 tables (preserving structure) and model_level at base-model granularity with config_level for capacity variants.\n\nHmm. Let me think about what's most useful and consistent with the schema. The schema wants:\n- matrix_tables: preserve multi-dim tables → definitely include all 7 base tables.\n- model_level: attribute × model matrix. If model = configuration, then values per configuration.\n\nGiven 27 configs, I think it's cleaner to define models at the configuration level with unique model_ids. Let me define model_ids as base model + Max + load cell capacity where needed for uniqueness:\n\nActually, let me reconsider: Maybe simpler to set models at base-model level (7 models: BSF-99, BSF-1214, BSG-99, BSG-1214, BS-1824, BS-2020, BS-2424) and use config_level with axis \"capacity_variant\" for Max/Min/e/T/load_cell per config. That uses config_level as intended: \"attribute × config-axis matrix\" with values [{condition, value}].\n\nconfig_level shape per schema example: values[]: {condition, value, unit_symbol?}. The rule 5 says values follow {condition, value}.\n\nSo:\ncharacteristics:\n- type_level: power supply, max scale intervals, max tare, max preset tare, excitation voltage, impedance min/max, min input voltage per interval, measuring range voltages, p_ind, temperature range, load cell connection.\n- config_level[]: \n  - attribute: maximum_capacity, axis: \"base_model_capacity_variant\", values: [{condition: \"BSF-99-3 (PW15AHC3MR/10 kg)\", value: 3, unit_symbol: \"kg\"}, ...]\n\nHmm, but that duplicates matrix_tables heavily. The schema says matrix_tables is for tables that don't fit elsewhere; config_level for attribute × config-axis. Both duplicating is acceptable per the R137 example which populates both matrix_tables AND model_level.\n\nGiven the R137 example populates both matrix_tables AND model_level, I should do similarly: matrix_tables for the 7 tables + model_level (or config_level) with the key attributes.\n\nDecision: I'll treat each base configuration as a \"model\" with unique model_id. This makes model_level straightforward: each attribute has 27 values.\n\nBut 27-item arrays × 5 attributes... acceptable. Actually wait — should I reduce? The most important varying attributes: maximum_capacity (Max), minimum_capacity (Min), verification_scale_interval (e), maximum_tare (T), load_cell model, load cell Emax. I'll include all six as model_level attributes.\n\nModel ID scheme: to keep unique and readable:\n1. \"BSF-99-3 (3 kg)\"\n2. \"BSF-99-6 (6 kg, PW15AHC3MR/10 kg)\"\n3. \"BSF-99-6 (6 kg, PW15AHC3MR/20 kg)\"\n4. \"BSF-1214-15 (6 kg)\"\n5. \"BSF-1214-15 (15 kg)\"\n6. \"BSF-1214-30 (30 kg)\"\n7. \"BSF-1214-45 (45 kg)\"\n8. \"BSF-1214-60 (60 kg)\"\n9. \"BSG-99-6 (3 kg)\"\n10. \"BSG-99-6 (6 kg)\"\n11. \"BSG-1214-15 (6 kg)\"\n12. \"BSG-1214-15 (15 kg)\"\n13. \"BSG-1214-30 (30 kg)\"\n14. \"BS-1824-45 (30 kg)\"\n15. \"BS-1824-45 (45 kg)\"\n16. \"BS-2020-50 (30 kg)\"\n17. \"BS-2020-45 (45 kg, FLS 125 lb)\"\n18. \"BS-2020-50 (45 kg)\"\n19. \"BS-2020-50 (50 kg)\"\n20. \"BS-2020-100 (60 kg)\"\n21. \"BS-2020-100 (90 kg)\"\n22. \"BS-2020-100 (100 kg)\"\n23. \"BS-2424-90 (90 kg)\"\n24. \"BS-2424-150 (150 kg)\"\n25. \"BS-2424-200 (200 kg, FLS R60 299 kg)\"\n26. \"BS-2424-200 (200 kg, FLS 1000 lb)\"\n27. \"BS-2424-250 (250 kg)\"\n\nHmm, this is getting messy. Alternative cleaner approach: keep models as the 7 base models and use config_level for capacity variants. Each config condition = \"BSF-99-3 (PW15AHC3MR/10 kg)\" etc.\n\nLet me weigh: The R137 example maps table rows to models. Here the table rows... each table has columns = configurations. Rows = attributes. So per table, columns are like models. So model = column config. I'll go with 27 models as configurations.\n\nActually, hmm, let me reconsider scope: maybe simpler and less error-prone to use base model as model_id and encode duplicates with suffixes like \"-A\"/\"-B\"? That fabricates designations. The certificate itself distinguishes them by Max and load cell. Using \"BSF-99-6 (Max 6 kg, Emax 10 kg)\" style is honest.\n\nI'll use format: \"BSF-99-6 / 6 kg / PW15AHC3MR-10kg\"? Too cryptic. Let me settle on:\n\nmodel_id: \"<BaseModel> (<Max> kg, LC <LoadCellCapacity>)\" where needed; simple \"<BaseModel> (<Max> kg)\" when unique within base.\n\nList:\n1. \"BSF-99-3 (3 kg)\" — LC PW15AHC3MR/10 kg, Emax 10 kg\n2. \"BSF-99-6 (6 kg, Emax 10 kg)\" — LC PW15AHC3MR/10 kg\n3. \"BSF-99-6 (6 kg, Emax 20 kg)\" — LC PW15AHC3MR/20 kg\n\nUsing Emax in the ID differentiates config 2/3 and 17 vs others. But config 18 \"BS-2020-50 (45 kg)\" vs 17 \"BS-2020-45 (45 kg)\" — base models differ, fine. 25 vs 26: \"BS-2424-200 (200 kg, Emax 299 kg)\" vs \"BS-2424-200 (200 kg, Emax 440 kg)\". OK use Emax disambiguator consistently only when needed.\n\nLoad cell model per config also goes in attributes (load_cell_model as string value). Attributes as model_level entries:\n- maximum_capacity (kg)\n- minimum_capacity (g)\n- verification_scale_interval_e (g)\n- maximum_tare (kg)\n- load_cell_maximum_capacity (kg)\n- load_cell_model (string)\n\nload_cell_model as string values: \"PW15AHC3MR/10 kg\" etc. Values must be structured objects with value... unit null, value as string.\n\nOK. Also \"T ≤ (kg)\" is maximum subtractive tare capacity. Label: maximum_tare. Value equals Max in all rows. I'll include.\n\nNow matrix_tables: 7 tables with name, columns, rows.\n\nTable 1: BSF-99 Torsion base (with HBM PW15AHC3MR load cell)\ncolumns: [\"base_model\", \"load_cell_model\", \"max\", \"min\", \"e\", \"max_tare\", \"load_cell_emax\"]\nHmm, the schema example uses column names + column_units. The example: columns: [\"size\", \"qmax\", \"qt\", ...], column_units: [...]. But rows as objects per row: {\"size\": \"G2.5\", \"qmax\": 4.0, ...}.\n\nBut our tables are transposed: rows are attributes, columns are configs. Should I transpose to config-per-row? For consistency with the R137 example (one row per model), yes, transpose: each row = one configuration.\n\nRow object: {\"base_model\": \"BSF-99-3\", \"load_cell_model\": \"PW15AHC3MR/10 kg\", \"max_kg\": 3, \"min_g\": 20, \"e_g\": 1, \"max_tare_kg\": 3, \"load_cell_emax_kg\": 10}\n\nHmm, but column_units are separate. Let me define columns: [\"base_model\", \"load_cell_model\", \"maximum_capacity\", \"minimum_capacity\", \"verification_scale_interval\", \"maximum_tare\", \"load_cell_maximum_capacity\"] and column_units: [null, null, \"kg\", \"g\", \"g\", \"kg\", \"kg\"]. Rows: objects with numeric values.\n\nTable 1 rows (3):\n{\"base_model\": \"BSF-99-3\", \"load_cell_model\": \"PW15AHC3MR/10 kg\", \"maximum_capacity\": 3, \"minimum_capacity\": 20, \"verification_scale_interval\": 1, \"maximum_tare\": 3, \"load_cell_maximum_capacity\": 10}\n{\"base_model\": \"BSF-99-6\", \"load_cell_model\": \"PW15AHC3MR/10 kg\", 6, 40, 2, 6, 10}\n{\"base_model\": \"BSF-99-6\", \"load_cell_model\": \"PW15AHC3MR/20 kg\", 6, 40, 2, 6, 20}\n\nTable 2: BSF-1214 Torsion base — 5 rows.\n{\"BSF-1214-15\", \"PW15AHC3MR/20 kg\", 6, 40, 2, 6, 20}\n{\"BSF-1214-15\", \"PW15AHC3MR/20 kg\", 15, 100, 5, 15, 20}\n{\"BSF-1214-30\", \"PW15AHC3MR/50 kg\", 30, 200, 10, 30, 50}\n{\"BSF-1214-45\", \"PW15AHC3MR/100 kg\", 45, 400, 20, 45, 100}\n{\"BSF-1214-60\", \"PW15AHC3MR/100 kg\", 60, 400, 20, 60, 100}\n\nTable 3: BSG-99 — 2 rows: load cell \"1 130-10 kg\" (normalize to \"1130-10 kg\"? Keep as \"1130-10 kg\"; the OCR shows \"1 130-10 kg\" with space. Vishay model is \"1130\". I'll write \"1130-10 kg\").\n{\"BSG-99-6\", \"1130-10 kg\", 3, 20, 1, 3, 10}\n{\"BSG-99-6\", \"1130-10 kg\", 6, 40, 2, 6, 10}\n\nTable 4: BSG-1214 — 3 rows:\n{\"BSG-1214-15\", \"1130-20 kg\", 6, 40, 2, 6, 20}\n{\"BSG-1214-15\", \"1130-20 kg\", 15, 100, 5, 15, 20}\n{\"BSG-1214-30\", \"1130-50 kg\", 30, 200, 10, 30, 50}\n\nTable 5: BS-1824 (1824 Diamond base) — 2 rows, LC \"FLS R60 59.5 kg\":\n{\"BS-1824-45\", \"FLS R60 59.5 kg\", 30, 200, 10, 30, 59.5}\n{\"BS-1824-45\", \"FLS R60 59.5 kg\", 45, 200, 10, 45, 59.5}\n\nTable 6: BS-2020 (2020 Diamond base) — 7 rows:\n{\"BS-2020-50\", \"FLS R60 59.5 kg\", 30, 200, 10, 30, 59.5}\n{\"BS-2020-45\", \"FLS 125 lb\", 45, 200, 10, 45, 59.7}\n{\"BS-2020-50\", \"FLS R60 59.5 kg\", 45, 200, 10, 45, 59.5}\n{\"BS-2020-50\", \"FLS R60 59.5 kg\", 50, 200, 10, 50, 59.5}\n{\"BS-2020-100\", \"FLS R60 119 kg\", 60, 400, 20, 60, 119}\n{\"BS-2020-100\", \"FLS R60 119 kg\", 90, 400, 20, 90, 119}\n{\"BS-2020-100\", \"FLS R60 119 kg\", 100, 400, 20, 100, 119}\n\nTable 7: BS-2424 (2424 Diamond base) — 5 rows:\n{\"BS-2424-90\", \"FLS 250 lb\", 90, 400, 20, 90, 113}\n{\"BS-2424-150\", \"FLS R60 299 kg\", 150, 1000, 50, 150, 299}\n{\"BS-2424-200\", \"FLS R60 299 kg\", 200, 1000, 50, 200, 299}\n{\"BS-2424-200\", \"FLS 1000 lb\", 200, 1000, 50, 200, 440}\n{\"BS-2424-250\", \"FLS R60 299 kg\", 250, 1000, 50, 250, 299}\n\nDescription for each table: base type and load cell family. E.g. \"BSF-99 Torsion base with HBM PW15AHC3MR load cell\".\n\nNow components: The cert names sub-assemblies: ZQ375 Indicating Device, bases (BSF/BSG torsion, BS Diamond), ZQ-BAT battery box (optional), ZQ-OPTO interface box (optional), load cells (HBM PW15AHC3MR, Vishay 1130, Avery FLS/FLS R60). Load cell alternatives: \"Any compatible load cell may be used\" with conditions. Components entries:\n- role: \"indicating device\", type_designations: [\"ZQ375\"], note: described in R76/2006-GB1-12.04.\n- role: \"load receptor base\", type_designations: [\"BSF-99\", \"BSF-1214\", \"BSG-99\", \"BSG-1214\", \"BS-1824\", \"BS-2020\", \"BS-2424\"] with alternatives null (all used across variants...). Actually BSF/BSG are Torsion; BS is Diamond. Could split into two components entries: \"torsion base\" and \"diamond base\".\n- role: \"load cell\" with alternatives: the three named load cells — HBM PW15AHC3MR, Vishay 1130, Avery FLS — plus \"any compatible R60 load cell\". alternatives: \"OR\". characteristics: conditions? The conditions listed for compatible load cells. I can add a \"conditions\" or use characteristics {compatible_load_cell_requirements...}. Keep simple: role \"load cell\", type_designations [\"HBM PW15AHC3MR\", \"Vishay 1130\", \"Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS\", \"FLS R60\"], alternatives \"OR\"