\"%\". OK.\n\nBut wait — maximum_tare also appears per-configuration as T ≤ (kg) in model_level. The -100% is a type-level limit. Keep both but different labels: type_level \"maximum_tare\" (-100 %), model_level \"maximum_tare_capacity\" (kg). Fine.\n\n- maximum_preset_tare: -100, \"%\"\n- load_cell_excitation_voltage: 5, \"V\"\n- load_cell_minimum_impedance: 58.33, \"Ω\"\n- load_cell_maximum_impedance: 1100, \"Ω\"\n- minimum_input_voltage_per_scale_interval: 0.8, \"µV\"\n- measuring_range_minimum_voltage: 0, \"mV\"\n- measuring_range_maximum_voltage: 15, \"mV\"\n- fraction_of_maximum_permisible_error: p_ind = 0.5 → attribute \"fraction_of_maximum_permisible_error_pind\": 0.5, unit null. Label per rule: \"fraction_of_maximum_permisible_error_p_ind\"? snake_case: p_ind is fine: \"apportionment... \" Actually rule examples: \"Apportionment factor pLC\" → \"apportionment_factor_plc\". So \"Fraction of maximum permissible error Pind\" → \"fraction_of_maximum_permisible_error_pind\". I'll use that.\n- operating_temperature_range: {min:-10, max:40}, \"°C\"\n- load_cell_connection: string \"4 or 6-core with braided outer screen, flexible PVC overall jacket, 0.5 mm² per core; maximum length (6-wire) = 30 m (60 m/mm²)\" — maybe split: load_cell_connection_type: \"4 or 6-core screened\", cable_cross_section: 0.5 \"mm²\", max_cable_length_6_wire: 30 \"m\" with note. I'll do:\n  - load_cell_connection: \"4 or 6-core with braided outer screen, flexible PVC overall jacket\" (string)\n  - load_cell_cable_cross_section: 0.5 mm²\n  - maximum_load_cell_cable_length_6_wire: 30 m (with footnote? no)\n- accuracy_class: \"III\" → type_level characteristic accuracy_class: value \"III\". Also recommendation.accuracy_classes: [\"III\"].\n- software: software_designation \"AWT30-500161\", software_version \"1.x.x.x or 2.x.x.x\".\n\nAlso zero-setting: devices \"Semi-automatic zero setting (≤ 4% Max)\" → could be characteristic zero_setting_device: value {max: 4} \"% of Max\"? I'll add: semi_automatic_zero_setting_range: {max:4} unit \"% of Max\"? Keep unit \"%\": value {min:null, max:4}, unit \"%\". Label: semi_automatic_zero_setting_device_max? Hmm. Simpler: include in devices list within description. I'll include two characteristics:\n- zero_setting_device_max: 4 \"% of Max\"? The unit \"% of Max\" isn't standard; use unit_symbol \"%\" and value {min:null,max:4}. attribute \"zero_setting_device_range\". Eh. I'll skip granular and mention devices in description to avoid over-engineering. Actually, these are legally relevant; but description covers them.\n\nTest reports:\n- id: \"P01332\", pages: 13, role: \"type_evaluation_report\", date: not given → omit date. \n- Also \"associated test report\" referenced — same P01332.\n\nRevision history:\n- {revision: \"0\"?} First issue: R76/2006-GB1-12.05, 27 April 2012, \"Certificate first issued\". revision: \"\" hmm. Use revision \"0\"? The table: Issue № R76/2006-GB1-12.05 (no rev) → I'll use \"0\"? Rule: revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0). For first issue without revision number, \"0\" is conventional. I'll use \"0\".\n- \"1\", 12 October 2012, \"USB Device added to the certificate.\"\n- \"2\", 22 August 2014, \"Company name changed from Avery Weigh-Tronix Ltd. to Avery Weigh-Tronix. Corrected errors in Technical Characteristics Table. Updated Metrological Characteristics to include additional capacities, bases and load cells, and correct an error with HBM load cell model number. Addition of Version 2.x.x.x software.\"\n\nFootnotes: none present. Omit.\n\nRecommendation:\n- id: \"R76\", edition: 2006, amendment: null, scheme: null (the R76 cert; scheme A/B refers to MAA? The certificate.scheme field at certificate level is A|B. This is an MAA certificate (scheme B). Should recommendation also have scheme? Schema shows recommendation: id, edition, amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes. I'll put scheme: \"B\"? Hmm, the recommendation-level scheme — for R76 the \"scheme\" might not apply. I'll include scheme \"B\" at certificate level only, and omit at recommendation level... Actually the schema lists scheme in both. For safety, include scheme: \"B\" in certificate; in recommendation include accuracy_classes [\"III\"], edition 2006, amendment null, id \"R76\". I might omit scheme there.\n\nWait — is this truly MAA? Footer: \"OIML R76 MAA Rev. 2\". Yes, MAA. Scheme B. Good.\n\nCertificate number: \"R76/2006-GB1-12.05\". Revision 2 — the number field: keep as \"R76/2006-GB1-12.05\". The revision is captured in revision_history and maybe number should be \"R76/2006-GB1-12.05 Rev. 2\"? Keep base number, revision implied by date_issued/history.\n\npage_total: 5 (as stated; the markdown shows up to Page 7 but that's OCR page artifacts — the doc says \"includes 5 pages\" consistently). Hmm wait, markdown shows \"Page 1 ... Page 7\" with \"This certificate includes 5 pages\" each time. The certificate claims 5 pages; the PDF-to-markdown produced 7 page markers (maybe extra). The document's own statement: 5. I'll use 5. Hmm, but observed pages = 7. The field is \"page_total\" of the document — document says 5. I'll go with 5.\n\nmember_state: \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\" — or \"United Kingdom\". The header says \"Member State of OIML United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\". I'll use \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\".\n\ndate_issued: \"2014-08-22\"? Schema doesn't specify format. I'll use ISO \"2014-08-22\". Or keep \"22 August 2014\". I'll use ISO for machine-readability. Hmm, risk: grader may expect the raw string. ISO is cleaner; either fine. I'll use \"2014-08-22\".\n\nNow the model_level matrices. 27 models. Let me define model list with IDs (I'll append distinguishing info only where duplicate base+max):\n\n1. BSF-99-3 (3 kg)\n2. BSF-99-6 (6 kg, Emax 10 kg)\n3. BSF-99-6 (6 kg, Emax 20 kg)\n4. BSF-1214-15 (6 kg)\n5. BSF-1214-15 (15 kg)\n6. BSF-1214-30 (30 kg)\n7. BSF-1214-45 (45 kg)\n8. BSF-1214-60 (60 kg)\n9. BSG-99-6 (3 kg)\n10. BSG-99-6 (6 kg)\n11. BSG-1214-15 (6 kg)\n12. BSG-1214-15 (15 kg)\n13. BSG-1214-30 (30 kg)\n14. BS-1824-45 (30 kg)\n15. BS-1824-45 (45 kg)\n16. BS-2020-50 (30 kg)\n17. BS-2020-45 (45 kg)\n18. BS-2020-50 (45 kg)\n19. BS-2020-50 (50 kg)\n20. BS-2020-100 (60 kg)\n21. BS-2020-100 (90 kg)\n22. BS-2020-100 (100 kg)\n23. BS-2424-90 (90 kg)\n24. BS-2424-150 (150 kg)\n25. BS-2424-200 (200 kg, Emax 299 kg)\n26. BS-2424-200 (200 kg, Emax 440 kg)\n27. BS-2424-250 (250 kg)\n\nCheck uniqueness: 14 vs 16: \"BS-1824-45 (30 kg)\" vs \"BS-2020-50 (30 kg)\" unique. 15 \"BS-1824-45 (45 kg)\", 17 \"BS-2020-45 (45 kg)\", 18 \"BS-2020-50 (45 kg)\" unique. Good. 25/26 differ by Emax. Good.\n\nmodel_level attributes:\n- maximum_capacity: kg values per model (27 values)\n- minimum_capacity: g\n- verification_scale_interval: g (e)\n- maximum_tare_capacity: kg (T ≤)\n- load_cell_maximum_capacity: kg (Emax of LC)\n- load_cell_model: string\n\nLet me now write values per model in order:\n\nmaximum_capacity (kg):\n1:3, 2:6, 3:6, 4:6, 5:15, 6:30, 7:45, 8:60, 9:3, 10:6, 11:6, 12:15, 13:30, 14:30, 15:45, 16:30, 17:45, 18:45, 19:50, 20:60, 21:90, 22:100, 23:90, 24:150, 25:200, 26:200, 27:250\n\nminimum_capacity (g):\n1:20, 2:40, 3:40, 4:40, 5:100, 6:200, 7:400, 8:400, 9:20, 10:40, 11:40, 12:100, 13:200, 14:200, 15:200, 16:200, 17:200, 18:200, 19:200, 20:400, 21:400, 22:400, 23:400, 24:1000, 25:1000, 26:1000, 27:1000\n\nverification_scale_interval (g):\n1:1, 2:2, 3:2, 4:2, 5:5, 6:10, 7:20, 8:20, 9:1, 10:2, 11:2, 12:5, 13:10, 14:10, 15:10, 16:10, 17:10, 18:10, 19:10, 20:20, 21:20, 22:20, 23:20, 24:50, 25:50, 26:50, 27:50\n\nmaximum_tare_capacity (kg): equals maximum_capacity list.\n\nload_cell_maximum_capacity (kg):\n1:10, 2:10, 3:20, 4:20, 5:20, 6:50, 7:100, 8:100, 9:10, 10:10, 11:20, 12:20, 13:50, 14:59.5, 15:59.5, 16:59.5, 17:59.7, 18:59.5, 19:59.5, 20:119, 21:119, 22:119, 23:113, 24:299, 25:299, 26:440, 27:299\n\nload_cell_model (strings):\n1: \"HBM PW15AHC3MR/10 kg\" — actually the table says \"PW15AHC 3MR/10 kg\" (OCR spacing). Normalize \"PW15AHC3MR/10 kg\".\n2: \"PW15AHC3MR/10 kg\"\n3: \"PW15AHC3MR/20 kg\"\n4: \"PW15AHC3MR/20 kg\"\n5: \"PW15AHC3MR/20 kg\"\n6: \"PW15AHC3MR/50 kg\"\n7: \"PW15AHC3MR/100 kg\"\n8: \"PW15AHC3MR/100 kg\"\n9: \"Vishay 1130-10 kg\" → \"1130-10 kg\"\n10: \"1130-10 kg\"\n11: \"1130-20 kg\"\n12: \"1130-20 kg\"\n13: \"1130-50 kg\"\n14: \"FLS R60 59.5 kg\"\n15: \"FLS R60 59.5 kg\"\n16: \"FLS R60 59.5 kg\"\n17: \"FLS 125 lb\"\n18: \"FLS R60 59.5 kg\"\n19: \"FLS R60 59.5 kg\"\n20: \"FLS R60 119 kg\"\n21: \"FLS R60 119 kg\"\n22: \"FLS R60 119 kg\"\n23: \"FLS 250 lb\"\n24: \"FLS R60 299 kg\"\n25: \"FLS R60 299 kg\"\n26: \"FLS 1000 lb\"\n27: \"FLS R60 299 kg\"\n\nFor load_cell_model, unit null. For base family (BSF with HBM, BSG with Vishay, BS with Avery) — the manufacturer prefix: HBM PW15AHC3MR, Vishay 1130, Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS. I'll include manufacturer in load_cell_model string: \"HBM PW15AHC3MR/10 kg\", \"Vishay 1130-10 kg\", \"Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS R60 59.5 kg\"? That's verbose; keep plain designations but add component entries naming manufacturers. I'll keep plain: \"PW15AHC3MR/10 kg\", \"1130-10 kg\", \"FLS R60 59.5 kg\", \"FLS 125 lb\", \"FLS 250 lb\", \"FLS 1000 lb\".\n\nHmm — components: add load cell entries with manufacturers:\ncomponents:\n- role: \"indicating device\", type_designations: [\"ZQ375\"], characteristics: {described_in: \"R76/2006-GB1-12.04\"}? Keep characteristics minimal. Maybe include \"documentation_reference\".\n- role: \"base / load receptor\", type_designations: [\"BSF-99\", \"BSF-1214\", \"BSG-99\", \"BSG-1214\", \"BS-1824\", \"BS-2020\", \"BS-2424\"]\n- role: \"load cell\", alternatives \"OR\", type_designations: [\"HBM PW15AHC3MR\", \"Vishay 1130\", \"Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS\", \"Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS R60\"], characteristics: {\"compatibility\": \"Any compatible load cell may be used provided it holds an OIML R60 Certificate of Conformity covering the type and required data; NH-marked load cells allowed only if humidity-tested to R76; compatibility established via modules calculation at verification; standard transmission type.\"}\n- role: \"optional battery box\", type_designations: [\"ZQ-BAT\"]\n- role: \"optional interface box\", type_designations: [\"ZQ-OPTO\"], characteristics: {assembly: \"with or without beacon assembly\"}\n\ncertified_type.description: summarize: \"Family designated ZQ375 Checkweigher comprising Torsion base (BSF/BSG) and Diamond base (BS) models. Class III, mains or battery powered, self-indicating non-automatic weighing instrument, not designed for direct sales to the public. Devices include semi-automatic zero setting (≤4% Max), zero tracking (≤4% Max), semi-automatic subtractive tare, pre-set tare, ... checkweighing applications (Sim375, Mid375, Adv375, Per375, Grad375). Interfaces: RS232/422/485, current loop, Ethernet, USB host/device, wireless LAN 802.11b/g, logic inputs, open collector outputs.\"\n\nSoftware protection note too.\n\nType-level characteristics — build JSON:\n\n\"power_supply_voltage\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 110, \"max\": 240}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}\n\"power_supply_type\": {\"value\": \"AC\", \"unit_symbol\": null, ...}\n\"power_supply_frequency\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\"}\n\"maximum_number_of_scale_intervals\": {\"value\": 6000, \"unit_symbol\": null}\n\"maximum_tare\": {\"value\": -100, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n\"maximum_preset_tare\": {\"value\": -100, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n\"load_cell_excitation_voltage\": {\"value\": 5, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"}\n\"load_cell_minimum_impedance\": {\"value\": 58.33, \"unit_symbol\": \"Ω\"}\n\"load_cell_maximum_impedance\": {\"value\": 1100, \"unit_symbol\": \"Ω\"}\n\"minimum_input_voltage_per_scale_interval\": {\"value\": 0.8, \"unit_symbol\": \"µV\"}\n\"measuring_range_minimum_voltage\": {\"value\": 0, \"unit_symbol\": \"mV\"}\n\"measuring_range_maximum_voltage\": {\"value\": 15, \"unit_symbol\": \"mV\"}\n\"fraction_of_maximum_permisible_error_p_ind\": {\"value\": 0.5, \"unit_symbol\": null} — label snake: \"fraction_of_maximum_permisible_error_pind\". I'll use \"fraction_of_maximum_permisible_error_pind\".\n\"operating_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -10, \"max\": 40}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n\"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": \"III\", ...}\n\"software_designation\": {\"value\": \"AWT30-500161\"}\n\"software_version\": {\"value\": \"1.x.x.x or 2.x.x.x\"}\n\"load_cell_connection\": {\"value\": \"4 or 6-core with braided outer screen, flexible PVC overall jacket\", ...}\n\"load_cell_cable_cross_section\": {\"value\": 0.5, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm²\"}\n\"maximum_load_cell_cable_length\": {\"value\": 30, \"unit_symbol\": \"m\"} with condition 6-wire — maybe fold note: add \"load_cell_cable_length_reference\": \"6-wire (60 m/mm²)\"? I'll add footnote-ish characteristic: \"maximum_load_cell_cable_length_6_wire\": 30 m. OK.\n\nShould each structured value include \"footnote_markers\": []? Schema shows footnote_markers as part of value object; include empty array or omit. The example shows footnote_markers:[\"1)\"] when present. I'll include \"footnote_markers\": [] for consistency. Hmm, rule 2 shape includes footnote_markers key. I'll include it always (empty when none).\n\nConfig_level: Not needed if model_level covers everything. The base-model axis is captured via matrix_tables. I'll skip config_level.\n\nNow certificate.scheme: \"B\" (MAA). project_number: \"TS1201/0041\"? I'm torn. The \"Reference №: TS1201/0041\" appears next to Issue Date on the certificate. It's the NMO reference for this certification project. Mapping to project_number preserves data. I'll include it. Hmm — could equally be omitted. I'll include: \"project_number\": \"TS1201/0041\". Reasonable.\n\noiml_issuer_id: not present → omit.\n\nIssuing authority address: \"Stanton Avenue, Teddington, TW11 0JZ, United Kingdom\" → address_lines: [\"Stanton Avenue\", 